          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the most reliable refrigerator brand in 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, Whirlpool and GE are the most reliable refrigerator brands, because their simple top-freezer and bottom-freezer models have few parts to fail and the largest repair networks behind them. Bosch is the strongest choice for a counter-depth French door. Configuration still matters more than brand: skip the door ice maker for the fewest repairs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which refrigerator brands should I avoid?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No mainstream brand needs to be avoided outright, but LG and Samsung have the most documented trouble, through lawsuits over LG linear compressors and Samsung door ice makers. If you buy either, choose a model without a door ice maker, register it, and keep the receipt. Check any model number for recalls before buying.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What type of refrigerator lasts the longest?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Top-freezer refrigerators last longest and need the fewest repairs, because they have the simplest airflow, one evaporator and usually no dispenser. Bottom-freezer models with the ice maker inside the freezer drawer come close. French door models with an ice maker in the fresh-food door are the most repair-prone style.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a refrigerator last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About 10 to 15 years. A widely cited National Association of Home Builders study put the average near 13 years. Simple models often run longer, while feature-heavy ones tend to need repairs sooner. Cleaning the condenser coils and keeping the door gaskets sealed both help a fridge reach the top of that range.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth repairing a 10-year-old refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes for a small part such as an ice maker, fan motor, defrost heater or door gasket. Usually no for a failed compressor or refrigerant leak. A common rule is to replace the fridge if the repair costs more than half the price of a comparable new one and the fridge is past two-thirds of its expected life.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are refrigerators with ice makers less reliable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Consumer Reports has long advised that refrigerators with ice makers and dispensers are more likely to need repairs, and technicians report the same pattern. The in-door versions on French door models are the most trouble because they need their own chilled duct. An ice maker inside the freezer is a safer compromise.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is GE or Whirlpool more reliable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are close, and both rank at the top here. Whirlpool has the larger parts supply and shares parts across Maytag and Amana, which makes repairs easy. GE runs its own factory service network across most of the country. Pick whichever has the better-rated authorized technician near you, and choose the simpler model of the two.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
